      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Eudora’s name came from Eudora Springs, a mineral‑spring resort that briefly drew visitors in the 1890s before agriculture took over.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eudora_missouri",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Kansas City, Pittsburg and Gulf Railroad arrived in the 1890s, turning the former Gulf/Sharon outpost into a small station community that linked the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eudora_missouri",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A post office operated in Eudora from 1900 until 1964, after which mail service moved to a rural station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/eudora_missouri",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The community lies near the Pomme de Terre River, whose flow supports local drainage and groundwater recharge in the county’s karst system.",
